---
import NotBuilt from '../components/NotBuilt.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { NEW_ISSUE_URL } from '../lib/site.ts';
---

<Base
  title="Authors"
  description="Author mode will let a mod author port their own mod to another loader as a pull request. It is not built yet (milestone M4)."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <PageIntro
      eyebrow="Author mode"
      title="For mod authors"
      lead="Publishing a port is your call. Author mode will make it easy for you, and only for you."
    />

    <NotBuilt milestone="M4" what="Author mode">
      <p>There is no sign-in yet, and no pull requests are opened by this project.</p>
      <p>
        Author mode starts with the first direction, Fabric → NeoForge. Each other direction joins once it has its
        own board column, in the order on the <a href="/docs/directions/">Directions</a> page.
      </p>
    </NotBuilt>

    <div class="prose body">
      <h2>What it will do</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          You sign in with GitHub. The site lists your repositories that contain a loader manifest, such as
          <code>fabric.mod.json</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          You pick a repository and the loader you want a port for. "Port this" runs a Tier B conversion on the
          latest release JAR of that repository.
        </li>
        <li>
          It opens a pull request on a new branch of your repository with the converted build setup (a Gradle
          subproject or a source set for the target loader, such as <code>neoforge/</code>, when decompilation
          succeeded cleanly), the parity report, and a badge snippet for your README.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Opting out</h2>
      <p>
        An author who verifies ownership can mark a mod "do not port". The converter will then refuse it in every
        direction, the board will hide it, and the refusal will say why. This holds even for permissive licenses.
      </p>
      <p>
        The verified opt-out flow arrives with author mode. Until then you can file a request with the
        <code>author-opt-out</code> issue form on <a href={NEW_ISSUE_URL}>GitHub</a>. Nothing converts today,
        so there is nothing to refuse yet; requests on record must be honoured by the board and the converter
        from their first run.
      </p>
      <p>Read the full <a href="/docs/legal/">legal and license policy</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
